\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \'95	Summarize the differences between how Services are initialized in 
\f2 JavaApplication
\f1  and 
\f2 DiscodeitApplication
\f1 , focusing on the following keywords:\
\pard\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\partightenfactor0
\cf0 1.	IoC Container\
2.	Dependency Injection\
\pard\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf0 3.	Bean\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 1-1. IoC Container (Inversion of Control Container)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 -> JavaApplication: The developer directly creates and manages objects.\
-> DiscodeitApplication: Spring\'92s IoC Container is responsible for creating and managing objects.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1-2. Dependency Injection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 -> JavaApplication: The developer directly provides the required dependency objects, as in 
\f2 new BasicUserService(userRepository)
\f1 .\
-> DiscodeitApplication: Spring automatically injects the Repository dependencies required by each Service.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1-3. Bean\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 -> JavaApplication: Objects created directly by the developer are not registered as Spring Beans.\
-> DiscodeitApplication: Objects registered in the Spring Container through annotations such as 
\f2 @Service
\f1  and 
\f2 @Repository
\f1  are called Beans.\
